有一个非常著名的例子，一位左翼活动家，普林斯顿大学教授理查德·福尔克（Richard Falk），他在《纽约时报》上写过关于他的文章。他说，当阿亚图拉上台时，他很可能会为第三世界国家提供一种急需的人道治理模式。福柯也是他的大粉丝，不是吗？是的，这就是你此时对他得到的评论。现在，霍梅尼传回伊朗的信息发出了非常不同的声音。你读了其中一条，在本集开头读了最关键的一条，

49
00:24:47.000 --> 00:25:23.000
which was his jolly New Year message smuggled into Iran for the opening of Muharram. Now, Muharam is the first month in the Islamic calendar, and it's really important and holy for Shia. isn't it? Because it begins with nine days of mourning. And the 10th day is Ashura, which is the day that we talked about in the previous episode, which is the anniversary of the death of Hussein at the Battle of Kabul, which is seen as this kind of cosmic moment of disaster. Exactly. And so Homini sends this message, and he says, this is the month. This is the month that will be famous throughout history
那是他为穆哈拉姆月开端而走私到伊朗的快乐新年信息。现在，穆哈拉姆月是伊斯兰历的第一个月，对什叶派来说非常重要和神圣，不是吗？因为它以九天的哀悼开始。第10天是阿舒拉节，这是我们在上一集中谈到的日子，也就是侯赛因在卡尔巴拉战役中死亡的周年纪念日，这被视为一种宇宙灾难时刻。完全正确。所以霍梅尼发出了这个信息，他说，这就是这个月。这就是将在历史上闻名的月份，

50
00:25:23.000 --> 00:25:59.000
when the freedom fighters will clench their fists and win. You know, when people should sacrifice their blood, he says, if you have to kill 5,000 people, 10,000 people, 20,000 people, do whatever it takes to bring down the Shah. And this rhetoric is revolutionary, but it's also apocalyptic. The language of blood and sacrifice is hardwired into Shiite thinking. And it strikes a massive chord because for the next nine days, for the first nine days of morning, there are thousands of people dressed in white mourning shrouds out on the streets. I mean, Western reporters watching this said
当自由战士将紧握拳头并获胜时。你知道，当人们应该牺牲他们的鲜血时，他说，如果你必须杀死5000人、10000人、20000人，不惜一切代价推翻沙阿。这种修辞是革命性的，但也是末日论的。鲜血和牺牲的语言深深植根于什叶派思想中。它引起了巨大的共鸣，因为在接下来的九天里，在前九天的哀悼中，有成千上万的人穿着白色的哀悼寿衣走上街头。我的意思是，观看这一幕的西方记者说

51
00:25:59.000 --> 00:26:16.000
there were like an army of the dead or something. It was an extraordinary scene. All of these people dressed in white chanting against the Shah. There are nightly confrontations with the Shah's soldiers every evening. There are tanks and armoured cars in the streets. I mean, you talk about apocalypse, Tom.
他们就像一支死者大军之类的。这是一个非凡的场景。所有这些人穿着白衣，高呼反对沙阿的口号。每天晚上都有与沙阿士兵的对抗。街上有坦克和装甲车。我的意思是，你谈论末日，汤姆。

52
00:26:16.000 --> 00:26:54.000
There's a massive sense of apocalyptic dread in December 1978 on the streets of Iran. And then, just as the Ayatollah had foreseen, the climax comes, on Ashura, so the night of the 10th into the 11th of December, which is your anniversary of the Battle of Cabela and 680. And basically what happens is the Shah's army seals off the kind of ceremonial core of the city, the palaces, the ministers, the embassies, and they abandon the rest of the city to the crowds. And there are some estimates that there are a million people in the streets chanting Magbar Shah,
1978年12月，伊朗街头有一种巨大的末日恐惧感。然后，正如阿亚图拉所预见的那样，高潮在阿舒拉节到来，也就是12月10日到11日的晚上，这是你提到的680年卡尔巴拉战役的周年纪念日。基本上发生的事情是，沙阿的军队封锁了城市的仪式核心，宫殿、部长、大使馆，他们把城市的其余部分留给了人群。据估计，街上有百万人高呼“打倒沙阿”，

53
00:26:54.000 --> 00:27:26.000
Mark Bar, America, death to the Shah, death to America and all this kind of thing. Now, the fact that they're chanting death to America tells you that they think the Americans are the Shars. They're the embodiment of Westernization and secularism. They're the puppet masters. But the irony is if they are puppet masters, they are useless puppet masters. It's because they can't agree what to do. And we ended the last episode with Ambassador William Sullivan standing there in the U.S. Embassy. and thinking, this is a flipping nightmare.
“打倒美国”，所有这些东西。现在，他们高呼“打倒美国”的事实告诉你，他们认为美国人是沙阿的后台。他们是西方化和世俗主义的化身。他们是傀儡大师。但讽刺的是，如果他们是傀儡大师，他们也是无用的傀儡大师。因为他们无法就该做什么达成一致。我们在上一集的结尾是威廉·沙利文（William Sullivan）大使站在美国大使馆里，

54
00:27:26.000 --> 00:27:39.000
It's all going to pieces. We're going to have to change horses. We should find out what the Ayatala wants. He doesn't like communism. We don't like communism. We can do a deal. Right, there's some middle ground. Let us build bridges with the Islamic clerics.
心想，这真是一场彻头彻尾的噩梦。一切都在分崩离析。我们必须换马了。我们应该找出阿亚图拉想要什么。他不喜欢共产主义。我们也不喜欢共产主义。我们可以达成协议。对，有一些共同点。让我们与伊斯兰神职人员建立桥梁。

55
00:27:39.000 --> 00:27:54.000
We don't know anything about Khomeini. Come on. The window of time is closing. Let's sort this out. This goes down incredibly badly in Washington, especially with the hawkish national security advisor, Zabigny of Brzezinski. Brzezinski says to Carter,
我们对霍梅尼一无所知。拜托。时间窗口正在关闭。让我们解决这个问题。这在华盛顿引起了极大的反感，特别是对鹰派国家安全顾问兹比格涅夫·布热津斯基。布热津斯基对卡特说，

56
00:27:54.000 --> 00:28:13.000
Your bloke in Tehran has totally lost his marbles. But the Shah is our man. We should stick with the Shah. Get the Shah to send in the troops. Clear the streets. Let's stop messing around. They have a showdown and the Oval Office on the 10th of December. Cyrus Vance, the patrician kind of boarding school guy.
你在德黑兰的人完全疯了。但沙阿是我们的人。我们应该坚持支持沙阿。让沙阿派兵。清理街道。别再胡闹了。他们在12月10日的椭圆形办公室进行了一次摊牌。赛勒斯·万斯，那个贵族寄宿学校出身的家伙。

57
00:28:13.000 --> 00:28:27.000
He says, I think we should actually listen to Sullivan. He knows what he's talking about. He's my man from the State Department. Prisinski says, are you mad? You'd plot with some mad medieval fanatic against our own close ally. I've got to put this in the notes. It's an amazing story.
他说，我认为我们实际上应该听听沙利文的。他知道他在说什么。他是我的国务院的人。布热津斯基说，你疯了吗？你会和一个疯狂的中世纪狂热分子密谋反对我们自己的亲密盟友。我必须把这个写进笔记里。这是一个惊人的故事。

58
00:28:27.000 --> 00:28:41.000
Brzezinski got an interview from Le Mans, translated and gave it to Carter. And Carter read it, and he sent it back with one word written in the margin. Nutty! This wasn't peanut diplomacy. Such a missed opportunity.
布热津斯基从《世界报》（Le Monde）得到了一篇采访，翻译后给了卡特。卡特读了它，并把它退了回来，在页边空白处写了一个词：疯子（Nutty）！这不是花生外交。真是错失良机。

59
00:28:41.000 --> 00:28:57.000
This was his psychiatric diagnosis of the Ayatollah. And so Carter said, the Ayatollah is obviously a madman. Like, we can't do a deal with him. Now, Sullivan, when he heard the news, went mad in Tehran. and he sent off an incredibly insolent telegram back to Washington,
这是他对阿亚图拉的精神病诊断。所以卡特说，阿亚图拉显然是个疯子。就像，我们不能和他达成协议。现在，沙利文听到这个消息后，在德黑兰发疯了，

60
00:28:57.000 --> 00:29:23.000
and he said Carter has made a gross and perhaps irretrievable mistake by failing to send an emissary to Paris to see Khomeini. I cannot repeat, cannot understand the rationale. I'm going to say it right now. Ambassador Sullivan was undoubtedly right. I think it is a colossal mistake from Jimmy Carter not to have sent an emissary to see Khomeini. Maybe Khomeini would still have ended up in power,
他发了一封极其无礼的电报回华盛顿，他说卡特犯了一个严重且可能无法挽回的错误，因为他没有派特使去巴黎见霍梅尼。我无法，不能理解其中的逻辑。我现在就要说，沙利文大使无疑是正确的。我认为吉米·卡特没有派特使去见霍梅尼是一个巨大的错误。也许霍梅尼最终还是会掌权，

61
00:29:23.000 --> 00:29:48.000
and it would still have the Islamic Republic of Iran and whatever, but the Americans missed a window there. But if you have no understanding of the potential power that Islam and therefore the Ayatollah, as someone who is channeling the power of Islam has, you can understand why you would just say, well, he's nutty. That's what your ambassador is for. Your ambassador is there to get the temperature on the streets,
而且它仍然会有伊朗伊斯兰共和国之类的东西，但美国人错失了一个窗口。但如果你不了解伊斯兰教，以及因此作为传达伊斯兰教力量的人——阿亚图拉所拥有的潜在力量，你就能理解为什么你会说，好吧，他是个疯子。这就是你大使的作用。你的大使在那里是为了感受街头的温度，

62
00:29:48.000 --> 00:30:04.000
to understand what's going on. I mean, Sullivan wasn't perfect, but he had a sense things are spiraling out of control. I accept that, but I'm just sticking up for Jimmy Carter here, that he doesn't know what he's dealing with. Okay, he definitely doesn't know what he's dealing with. Anyway, Carter wanted to sack Sullivan straight away.
去了解正在发生什么。我的意思是，沙利文并不完美，但他感觉到事情正在失控。我接受这一点，但我只是在为吉米·卡特辩护，他不知道自己在处理什么。好吧，他绝对不知道自己在处理什么。总之，卡特想立刻解雇沙利文。

63
00:30:04.000 --> 00:30:26.000
And Cyrus Van said to him, we actually need to keep an ambassador and we can't be sacking our ambassador at this crucial moment. Anyway, the next day, Sullivan goes to the palace to see the Shah. And it's an extraordinary scene, a ghostly scene, the place is deserted. A lot of the Shah's courtiers have already fled abroad. they've been taking crates of furniture and jewelry and paintings and so on.
赛勒斯·万斯对他说，我们实际上需要留住一位大使，我们不能在这个关键时刻解雇我们的大使。总之，第二天，沙利文去皇宫见国王。那是一个非凡的场景，一个幽灵般的场景，宫殿空无一人。国王的许多朝臣已经逃往国外。他们带走了成箱的家具、珠宝、绘画等等。

64
00:30:26.000 --> 00:30:54.000
They've been making bank transfers to the Cayman Islands or they're all gotten gains, all of this. But the Shah and Empress Farah are still there in this kind of marble prison of the palace, surrounded by mirrors, these sort of sad figures dining by candle-like as the power cuts. Sullivan goes in and he says to the Shah, look, we had a plan to contact Comini. I mean, you might not like it, but we did. And we've scrapped it. and the Shah is shocked because he has always believed, weirdly,
他们一直在向开曼群岛进行银行转账，或者他们所有的不义之财，所有这些。但国王和法拉赫皇后仍然待在宫殿这座大理石监狱里，被镜子包围着，这些悲伤的人物在停电时借着烛光用餐。沙利文走进去，对国王说，看，我们曾有一个联系霍梅尼的计划。我的意思是，你可能不喜欢它，但我们确实有。而且我们已经取消了它。国王很震惊，因为他一直奇怪地相信，

65
00:30:54.000 --> 00:31:11.000
this thing about the Americans being puppet masters, he's believed it too. And he said, what, you don't have any plan? Sullivan said, we don't have any plan now, I'm really sorry. I mean, it's a classic example, isn't it, of the way in which actually conspiracy theories are a comfort blanket? It reassures you that somebody knows what's going on. You mean the British art masterminding this?
关于美国人是傀儡操纵者这件事，他也相信了。他说，什么，你们没有任何计划？沙利文说，我们现在没有任何计划，我真的很抱歉。我的意思是，这是一个经典的例子，不是吗？阴谋论实际上是一种安慰毯？它让你确信有人知道发生了什么。你是说英国人在幕后策划这一切？

66
00:31:11.000 --> 00:31:24.000
It's a real disappointment. It is total anarchy. And Sullivan says in his memoirs, up to this point, the Shah had imagined that we had some grand design that was intended to save his country and perhaps somehow or other his dynasty.
这真是一个巨大的失望。这完全是无政府状态。沙利文在他的回忆录中说，直到这一点，国王还以为我们有一个宏大的设计，旨在拯救他的国家，也许以某种方式拯救他的王朝。

67
00:31:24.000 --> 00:31:44.000
And it's like the scales fall from the Shah's eyes. It suddenly became clear to him as it had to me that we had no design whatsoever and that our government's actions were being guided by some inexplicable whim. So Sullivan, it was a bad day for both of us. I mean, it's a particularly bad day for the Shah. So events are now moving very quickly indeed.
就像国王眼中的鳞片脱落了一样。他突然变得清楚了，就像我一样，我们根本没有任何设计，我们政府的行动是被某种无法解释的突发奇想所引导的。所以沙利文，这对我们俩来说都是糟糕的一天。我的意思是，这对国王来说尤其糟糕。所以事件现在确实发展得非常快。

68
00:31:44.000 --> 00:32:07.000
A lot of Americans have already left, but there are still 10,000 or so in Iran. But on the 23rd of December, the most senior oil executive in Iran, who was a guy called Paul Grimm, who actually managed and ran the oil fields. He was ambushed and shot by militants. He was the first foreigner to be killed in the revolution. And the oil companies at this stage say, okay, we're pulling the plug. We're getting the people out.
许多美国人已经离开了，但伊朗仍有大约一万人。但在12月23日，伊朗最高级别的石油高管，一个叫保罗·格里姆的人，他实际上管理和运营着油田。他被武装分子伏击并枪杀。他是革命中第一个被杀的外国人。石油公司在这一阶段说，好吧，我们不干了。我们要把人撤出来。

69
00:32:07.000 --> 00:32:35.000
So they start getting their workers' families out. And by the end of December 1978, the oil fields of Iran, which are so central to the world's economy, have been pretty much completely shut down. So now, insanely, there is petrol rationing in Iran's cities. Iran, this oil-rich country has no oil for its own people. And in Europe, oil prices are going up daily. They've gone up by about 20%. So not good news for Jim Callahan.
所以他们开始把工人的家属撤出来。到1978年12月底，对世界经济至关重要的伊朗油田几乎完全关闭了。所以现在，疯狂的是，伊朗的城市里出现了汽油配给。伊朗这个石油资源丰富的国家竟然没有石油供自己的人民使用。而在欧洲，油价每天都在上涨。它们已经上涨了大约20%。所以这对吉姆·卡拉汉来说不是好消息。

70
00:32:35.000 --> 00:32:55.000
Not good news. Although Britain is now, of course, about North Sea oil is about to come on stream for Britain. So oddly, Britain is one country that wasn't massively affected. Oh, well, that's a positive. Yeah. So we're the riguaners and all this. And on the last day of 1978, the Shah makes one final despairing effort to recapture the initiative. He sacks his military government.
不是好消息。当然，虽然英国现在北海石油即将投入生产。所以奇怪的是，英国是一个没有受到严重影响的国家。哦，好吧，那是个积极的一面。是的。所以我们是钻井工人，所有这些。在1978年的最后一天，国王做出了最后一次绝望的努力来夺回主动权。他解散了他的军事政府。

71
00:32:55.000 --> 00:33:24.000
He brings in a new prime minister who is a moderate liberal dissident called Shapur Bakhtyar. He's the kind of person who a few years earlier, he'd have been a nice progressive hero with his lovely moustache. But now, you know, he's just a sort of Shah crony. And he's been left behind. He's the Korenski of the Iranian Revolution. Now, at this point, the Shah is already thinking, I'm probably going to have to get out. The next time he meets Sullivan, he says, I realize I'm going to have to go. And Sullivan says, where do you want to go?
他带来了一位新的总理，一位名叫沙普尔·巴赫蒂亚尔的温和自由派异见人士。他就是那种几年前会是一个留着漂亮胡子的进步英雄的人。但现在，你知道，他只是国王的一个走狗。他已经被抛弃了。他是伊朗革命的克伦斯基。现在，在这一点上，国王已经在想，我可能不得不离开了。下一次他见到沙利文时，他说，我意识到我不得不走了。沙利文说，你想去哪里？

72
00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:46.000
And the Shah says, a terrible thing, actually. The worst thing he ever did. He says William Sullivan, I have a home in England, but I don't want to go there because the weather is so bad. But, I mean, that's great news for Britain, isn't it? Because it would actually have been terrible for him to come because... Do you know what? Jim Gallowhan, great friend of the rest of his history, he had already said he didn't want the show. Didn't want him to come.
国王说，实际上是一件可怕的事情。他做过的最糟糕的事情。他说威廉·沙利文，我在英国有一个家，但我不想去那里，因为天气太糟糕了。但是，我的意思是，这对英国来说是个好消息，不是吗？因为他来这里实际上会很糟糕，因为……你知道吗？吉姆·卡拉汉，我们历史其余部分的好朋友，他已经说过他不想要国王。不想让他来。

73
00:33:46.000 --> 00:34:09.000
like George V, refusing Nicholas II. Yes. So Sullivan says to him, would you like to come to the United States? Would you like me to organise an invitation for you? And he said, the Shah lent forward like a little boy and said, oh, would you? So Sullivan reports back to him and says, I've got a place for you. Great publishing magnate and former ambassador to Britain, Walter Annenberg. He's got a house in Palm Springs, California, and he says you can have that.
就像乔治五世拒绝尼古拉二世一样。是的。所以沙利文对他说，你想去美国吗？你想让我为你组织一个邀请吗？他说，国王像个小男孩一样向前倾身说，哦，你会吗？所以沙利文向他汇报说，我为你找了个地方。伟大的出版大亨和前驻英大使沃尔特·安嫩伯格。他在加利福尼亚州的棕榈泉有一栋房子，他说你可以住那里。

74
00:34:09.000 --> 00:34:33.000
I've been to it. Did you stay in it, though, Tom? I didn't stay in it. I was, but it was the Palm Springs Literary Festival, which I highly recommend. We had a little tour. Well, the Shah, he says, I'm going to go to Egypt first to see my mate, President Sadat, then I'll go from there to California. And Sullivan says, once they'd discussed this, and once the Shah had reconciled himself to it, he seemed relieved, even excited at the prospect. And it's interesting to think what would have happened had he ended up in California,
我去过那里。汤姆，你住过吗？我没住过。我当时在，但那是棕榈泉文学节，我强烈推荐。我们进行了一次小参观。好吧，国王说，我先去埃及见我的朋友萨达特总统，然后从那里去加利福尼亚。沙利文说，一旦他们讨论了这一点，一旦国王接受了这一点，他似乎感到宽慰，甚至对前景感到兴奋。想到如果他最终去了加利福尼亚会发生什么，这很有趣，

75
00:34:33.000 --> 00:34:59.000
because California becomes the great bolthole, doesn't it, for lots of upper and middle class Iranians. So it really would have been a king in exile there, yeah. So the plan is put in motion. On the morning of Friday the 19th of January, the Shah and his Empress leave the palace for the last time. The guards, it's an amazing scene. The guards are all lined up, a lot of them in tears. They get on a helicopter. It's going to take them to Tehran Airport. It's a freezing cold and windy day.
因为加利福尼亚成了许多伊朗上层和中产阶级的避难所，不是吗？所以他真的会成为那里的流亡国王，是的。所以计划付诸实施。1月19日星期五早上，国王和他的皇后最后一次离开宫殿。卫兵们，这是一个惊人的场景。卫兵们都排成一列，许多人泪流满面。他们登上了一架直升机。它将带他们去德黑兰机场。那是一个寒冷多风的日子。

76
